.SS "Character encoding and locale"
The interface of
.I ee
is U.S. English only.
Text is read, edited and written in the UTF-8 encoding, which is the
only supported character encoding.
.IR ee
distinguishes between byte offsets into the buffer, Unicode characters
and terminal display columns, so multibyte, double-width and combining
characters are moved over and displayed correctly.
Cursor movement and deletion operate on whole Unicode code points, not
on grapheme clusters.
The character-code prompt (^a) accepts a decimal Unicode code point;
surrogate code points (U+D800..U+DFFF) and values above U+10FFFF are
ignored, so the editor never inserts an invalid sequence.
.PP
Files are considered text only if they are valid UTF-8 and contain no
NUL byte.
A file containing a NUL byte is reported as "not a text file"; a file
containing malformed UTF-8 is reported as "not valid UTF-8" and is not
opened.
The same validation is applied to text read from a shell pipe.
This keeps the buffer invariant "every line is valid UTF-8" true at all
times, so the editor can never corrupt a file by silently accepting
bytes it cannot represent.
.PP
The locale is used for multibyte support only and must be UTF-8.
If the environment names a locale, that choice is honoured:
.IR ee
requires the resulting
.SM LC_CTYPE
codeset to be UTF-8 and refuses to start otherwise, rather than
operating with a text semantics that is incompatible with its UTF-8
buffer.
If no locale is configured at all, a well-known UTF-8 locale is
selected automatically.
Setting
.SM LANG ,
.SM LANGUAGE ,
.SM LC_ALL ,
.SM LC_MESSAGES
or any other locale variable never changes the interface language,
which is compiled in as U.S. English.
There is no byte-oriented mode.
Legacy encodings such as ISO-8859-1, Shift-JIS and Big-5 are not
supported.
.SH WARNINGS
The automatic paragraph formatting operation may be too slow for
slower systems.
.SH FILES
.PP
.I /usr/share/misc/init.ee
.br
.I $HOME/.init.ee
.br
.I .init.ee
